          "letter": "",
          "english": "Arum and Roksa placed the blame for students' lack of learning on a watered-down college curriculum and lowered undergraduate work standards. Although going to college is supposed to be a full-time job, students spent, on average, only 12 to 14 hours a week studying and many were skating through their semesters without doing a significant amount of reading and writing. Students who take more challenging classes and spend more time studying do learn more. But the priorities of many undergraduates are with extracurricular activities, playing sports, and partying and socializing.",
          "chinese": "阿鲁姆和罗克萨将学生学习不足归咎于缩水的大学课程和降低了标准的本科作业。尽管上大学本应是一项全职工作，但学生平均每周只花12至14个小时学习，许多人整个学期都没有完成多少重要的阅读和写作任务，便轻松混了过去。选修更具挑战性的课程并投入更多时间学习的学生，确实能学到更多东西。然而，许多本科生更看重课外活动、参加体育运动以及参加派对和社交。",
